-- title:
--   Descent of a symmetric square root to a finite stage
-- statement:
--   Let $S$ be a commutative ring, $A$ a scheme, $f : A \to \operatorname{Spec} S$ a morphism, $L$ a relative group law for $f$ (a functorial group structure on $S$-points of $A$ over varying test schemes), and let $hA$ assert that $f$ is smooth, proper, has connected fibres and admits a relative group law. Let $\mathcal L$ be a module on $A$ which is invertible, i.e. locally on $A$ its pullback is isomorphic to the unit; let $\mathfrak p$ be a prime of $S$ and $g_1 \in S \setminus \mathfrak p$. Let $C$ be an $S$-algebra which is also an algebra over $S[1/g_1] =$ `Localization.Away g₁`, compatibly, and finite as an $S[1/g_1]$-module, and let $C_0$ be an $S$- and $C$-algebra, compatibly, which is a localisation of $C$ at the submonoid generated by the image of $S \setminus \mathfrak p$. Write $A_{C_0}$ for the pullback of $f$ along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $S \to C_0$, with projections $\mathrm{pr}_1$ to $A$ and $\mathrm{pr}_2$ to $\operatorname{Spec} C_0$. Let $L_0$ be a relative group law for $\mathrm{pr}_2$ whose multiplication is compatible with that of $L$ after composing points with $\mathrm{pr}_1$, and let $\mathcal L_0$ be an invertible module on $A_{C_0}$ which is symmetric for $L_0$, meaning that the pullback of $\mathcal L_0$ along the inversion morphism $[-1]$ attached to $L_0$ and $\mathcal L_0$ become isomorphic over $\mathrm{pr}_2^{-1}(U)$ for some open $U$ around each point of $\operatorname{Spec} C_0$; assume further that in the same local-on-the-base sense $\mathrm{pr}_1^{*}\mathcal L$ and $\mathcal L_0 \otimes [-1]^{*}\mathcal L_0$ agree. The conclusion: there is $r_1 \in S \setminus \mathfrak p$ such that for every ring $C_1$ which is an $S$- and $C$-algebra, compatibly, and is a localisation of $C$ at the submonoid generated by the image of the powers of $r_1$, every ring homomorphism $\varphi_0 : C_1 \to C_0$ over $C$, every morphism $\rho_0 : A_{C_0} \to A_{C_1}$ compatible with the projections to $A$ and with the projections to the bases via $\operatorname{Spec} \varphi_0$, and every relative group law $L_1$ for $A_{C_1} \to \operatorname{Spec} C_1$ compatible with $L$ in the same way as $L_0$, there exists an invertible module $\mathcal L_1$ on $A_{C_1}$ which is symmetric for $L_1$, which satisfies the local-on-the-base isomorphism between $\mathrm{pr}_1^{*}\mathcal L$ and $\mathcal L_1 \otimes [-1]^{*}\mathcal L_1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} C_1$, and whose pullback $\rho_0^{*}\mathcal L_1$ is isomorphic to $\mathcal L_0$.
--
--   This is the descent step in the construction of symmetric square roots of a line bundle on an abelian scheme: a square root already available over the semilocal stage $C_0$ is shown to come, together with its symmetry and its square isomorphism (both only local on the base), from a single finite stage $C[1/r_1]$ with $r_1 \notin \mathfrak p$. It is used in the construction of the symmetric principal square root over such a stage, which feeds the polarisation data for good reduction of Jacobians.
